---
scope: src/service/parseBrainAide
alignment: aligned
description: "Single canonical reader for the host's brain config — enforces the published required-section contract and names exactly what is missing or malformed when a file falls short."
intent: >
  parseBrainAide is the one place in the project that reads the host's
  brain config file. Every downstream consumer — install, sync, runtime
  brain tool, provisioner — reads the same parsed shape from this reader
  rather than re-parsing the file. The reader enforces the required-section
  contract, published from one source it shares with the agents that
  create and repair brain configs, and reports precisely which sections
  are missing or what is malformed. Beyond that structure it preserves
  content verbatim and leaves meaning to the consumers that own it. Any
  backend's config that satisfies the contract parses.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every consumer of the brain config file reads it through this reader. The shape returned is the single source of truth for what the file contains.
    - Unwired slots are preserved verbatim through to consumers as the typed null they were written as. The reader does not coerce them to strings or substitute placeholders.
    - The reader returns a typed structured value with each named section as its own field, so consumers pull the slice they need without rescanning the file.
    - The required sections are a published contract with a single source, each paired with a one-line purpose, and this reader and every agent that creates or repairs a brain config read that same source. A hand-written config for any backend that satisfies the contract parses, whether or not it came from the bundled template.
    - A file that falls short yields a specific reason — every missing required section by name, or the precise structural fault — never a failure indistinguishable from an absent file.
  undesired:
    - A second reader of the brain config file anywhere in the project — a regex, a re-parse, a hand-rolled extraction — that competes with this one as a source of truth.
    - A literal-string sentinel encoded into the parsed result to stand in for an unwired slot. Unwired slots are typed null; sentinels reintroduce the regression of string-based wiring detection.
    - The reader rejecting, coercing, or validating content whose meaning belongs to a downstream consumer. Domain-specific rules live with the consumer that owns them, not in the reader.
    - The required-section list restated anywhere besides its single published source, so the contract this reader enforces and the one agents repair against drift apart.
    - A missing required section treated as optional, defaulted, or silently filled in by the reader. Absence is a failure the user repairs through the brain configuration command.
    - Acceptance hinging on bundled-template specifics such as backend identity or template wording, so a custom-backend config that satisfies the contract is rejected.
---
